A drain refusal names the type the author has to satisfy, composed.

Today the message factors the base type out of the phantom and puts them in
separate clauses — "column *app/lib/pend:Pending has 0 dischargers for <!open>".

A phantom on its own names a SIDE of a transition, not a thing. `<open!>` is what
a producer hands back; `<!open>` is what a consumer takes. Both spellings of the
same state appear in this program's output one line apart, with the base type
attached to neither, so the reader cannot tell they are the same state seen from
two ends.

`*app/lib/pend:Pending<!open>` is the exact string in the signature that would
satisfy the store — `~pub tor cancel { p: *Pending<!open> }` in lib/pend.kz.
Composed, it is a thing the author can search the library for. Split, there is
nothing to match against.

PIN: the composed type appears in the refusal. Where in the sentence, and whether
the prose clause survives alongside it, is free.
